    The software developer's brain should not, in fact can not, contain all history and explanations.

    The software source files should not, in fact can not, contain all history and explanations.

    Source control mechanisms should not, in fact can not, contain all history and explanations.

    This file is the index relating a software change to a unique identifier in a database.

------------------------------------------

    The description of a version of a software product under development includes information that
    explains the REASON changes were made.  The lists of reasons for changes to the software include
    keywords usable in the information system used to keep track of all of the changes everywhere.

    In support of its educational goals, TSC LLC invents the word "FIXIT" as a placeholder for the
    word chosen by the software development culture behind the ecosystem the developer works in.

    The assignment of a FIXIT identifier instantiates a database entry that now will live forever.
    The FSM of state changes to the FIXIT over Time are described by corporate project management.

    The FIXIT database has a primary selection value for each individual and unique item.
    References to the value are made throughout repository metadata, without repeating the content.

------------------------------------------ [edit:none]

    Every software development organization makes up a set of conventions that must be followed.

    FIXIT keyword definition adopted for this project within this organization:

        FppTnn   <format><project ID><M:missing | B:broken><integer 0-99>

    The first letter defines the format of the rest of the keyword.  Every keyword in the database
    that starts with 'F' will have exactly and completly the format described here.  This allows
    corporate project management to separately share a single database across the organization.

    For example, for experimental projects that may still require oversight and documentation:

        Xlllccssnn     <format><3-letter lab acronym><category><subcategory><integer 0-99>

